{"name":"Oubliette","hex":"#4f4944","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"oubliette","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dungeon only accessible by a trapdoor at the top. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oubliette"}],"entries":[{"title":"Oubliette (The X-Files)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Oubliette\" is the eighth episode of the third season of the American science fiction television series The X-Files, and originally aired on the Fox network on November 17, 1995. Written by Charles Grant Craig and directed by Kim Manners, \"Oubliette\" is a \"monster of the week\" story, unconnected to the series' wider mythology. It earned a Nielsen rating of 10.2, and was watched by 15.90 million people on its initial broadcast. The episode received positive reviews. Both the emotional nature of the story and David Duchovny's performance received positive critical attention."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oubliette_(The_X-Files)","wikidata":"Q7110237"},{"title":"Oubliette (video game)","blocks":[{"text":"Oubliette is a 1977 multiplayer roleplaying game created for the PLATO system. It was designed by Jim Schwaiger, together with John Gaby and Bancherd DeLong. It was influential in the design of Avatar (1979) and Wizardry (1981)."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oubliette_(video_game)","wikidata":"Q139855099"},{"title":"Dungeon","blocks":[{"text":"A dungeon is a type of prison, usually an underground room or cell, in which prisoners are held. Dungeons are generally associated with medieval castles. They are closely connected in concept both to the prison and the oubliette, and the words are sometimes used interchangeably. In the popular imagination, a dungeon is claustrophobic, strong, damp, and located in a castle under the direct control of a medieval lord. This intentional unpleasantness and personal control over a dungeon contrasts to the modern prison, which provides a civil function."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/BlarneyCastle5.jpg/330px-BlarneyCastle5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dungeon","wikidata":"Q89753"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Jewel_Staite.jpg/250px-Jewel_Staite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Jewel_Staite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Jewel_Staite.jpg/250px-Jewel_Staite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The producers thought that Jewel Staite—who was only 13 at the time—looked old enough to play the role of a 15-year-old girl.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJewel_Staite.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Mountseymour.jpg/330px-Mountseymour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Mountseymour.jpg/330px-Mountseymour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The episode was filmed at Mount Seymour because it was the only location that met all the conditions of the crew. ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMountseymour.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/David_Duchovny_2011_Shankbone.JPG/500px-David_Duchovny_2011_Shankbone.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/David_Duchovny_2011_Shankbone.JPG/1280px-David_Duchovny_2011_Shankbone.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/